+# Agent Note: Step preparation and guarded execution
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-16-step-preparation-and-guarded-execution.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+An external compatibility plugin needs to change an agent's tools when its selected provider changes. Prompt providers must see that selection before evaluating their tool schemas. File moves require guarded removal, and interactive command handles need to preserve subprocess ownership during cancellation.
+
+## Decision
+
+System-prompt assembly resolves its context and then awaits scoped preparation before reading providers. The resolved context and route are shallow-frozen; resolvers cannot replace scope or cancellation. Model selection captures its route at resolution, while previews do not advance the executing step's selection. The agent loop still owns logging and freezes the model request through its existing admission path.
+
+Filesystem providers explicitly advertise guarded single-file removal. The local backend checks version and regular-file type under its existing target lock; the sandbox backend checks the current path before delegating. Shell requests can open stdin instead of supplying fixed input. Background handles expose pipe writes and closure plus termination and waiting for the provider's managed range, including descendants after command exit.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Rewrite the frozen LLM request in a plugin.** This breaks reconstruction from logged history and places tool selection after prompt evaluation. Scoped preparation keeps compatibility policy outside the agent loop and runs before assembly.
+
+**Forward hidden tools through the registry.** Hidden tools are intentionally unavailable to execution. Compatibility tools use the calling agent's filesystem and shell services instead.
+
+**Run separate filesystem or subprocess implementations inside the plugin.** This duplicates sandbox enforcement and execution-world ownership. The generic services expose the missing operations without embedding Codex-specific policy in the host.
+
+## Consequences
+
+External plugins can change scoped registrations at step preparation. They must dispose those registrations, stop their owned commands, and distinguish previews from execution. Guarded removal is not a multi-file transaction. Interactive stdin is a pipe, not a pseudo-terminal; providers that do not implement it remain unsupported for interactive tools.
+
+The focused preparation, route-selection, guarded-removal and sandbox-removal tests pass on Windows. Existing broader filesystem tests require symbolic-link privileges; the broader PowerShell suite also has unresolved short-deadline failures in this environment. This change supplies no native Responses transport or multi-projection compaction protocol.
